<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Italo-Turkish War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>–<a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Italo-Turkish War of 1911–1912 was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Italy seized Tripolitania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, which became the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, along with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese">Dodecanese</a> islands. The war expanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a> and saw early military use of aircraft.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/italo-turkish-war.jpg" alt="Italo-Turkish War" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: L'Illustrazione italiana, 16 June 1912.; Bilinmiyor … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italo-Turkish_war_collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Italian claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> date back to the Ottoman defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the war of 1877–1878. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> in 1878 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the United Kingdom agreed to a French occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> and British control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, and the French told Italian diplomats that Tripoli would be a counterpart for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Italy made a secret agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in February 1887, and in 1902 signed a secret treaty with France giving freedom of intervention in Tripolitania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. In the 1909 Racconigi Bargain Russia acknowledged Italy's interest in Tripoli and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> published a declaration of its direct interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> on 29 September 1911, and military operations began with the bombardment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a> on 3 October. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> had sent their Libyan battalions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> and had no full army in Tripolitania; their officers travelled there secretly through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libyans">Libyans</a> were organised for the defence. Italian forces captured Tripoli, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobruk">Tobruk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derna">Derna</a>, Bengasi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> between 3 and 21 October, but suffered a defeat at Shar al-Shatt. The corps was then enlarged to 100,000 men, and the war became one of position, with the Italians unable to advance far inland.</p><p>The war saw early uses of new technology. On 23 October 1911 Capitano Carlo Piazza flew the first aerial reconnaissance mission, and on 1 November Sottotenente Giulio Gavotti dropped the first aerial bomb on Ottoman troops. At sea the Italian Navy, with seven times the tonnage of the Ottoman, sank Ottoman gunboats at Kunfuda Bay in January 1912 and ships at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> on 24 February. On land the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics, and the Italians answered with repressive measures including public hangings; in October 1911 Italian troops killed thousands of civilians in the Tripoli massacre.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ouchy">Treaty of Ouchy</a>, signed on 18 October 1912, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, and Italy agreed to return the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese">Dodecanese</a>. The vagueness of its text, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> kept the islands under Italian administration, and in 1923 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> recognised the Italian annexation at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a>. The islands became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> by treaty in 1947.</p><p>The war cost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> 1.3 billion lire, nearly a billion more than Giovanni Giolitti had estimated. Resistance by the Senussi continued, and Italian control of the interior was ineffective until the late 1920s, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-badoglio">Pietro Badoglio</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rodolfo-graziani">Rodolfo Graziani</a> waged pacification campaigns. Resistance ended after the execution of Omar Mukhtar on 15 September 1931. The war is considered a precursor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-league">Balkan League</a> attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in October 1912, starting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a> days before the treaty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Italo-Turkish War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the <b>Italo-Turkish War</b> of 1911–1912, Italian forces seized coastal Libya and the Dodecanese Islands in the Aegean, establishing a broader Mediterranean presence, although the Libyan interior was subdued only by the brutal campaigns of the 1920s and early 1930s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Italo-Turkish War</b> of 1911–1912 brought Libya and the Dodecanese.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The song returned to favour in the <b>Italo-Turkish War</b> and the trenches of World War I.</blockquote><a href="/songs/canto-degli-italiani">Il Canto degli italiani</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Italo-Turkish War</b> of 1911-1912, Italy occupied Rhodes and the other islands of the Dodecanese.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-islands-of-the-aegean">Italian Islands of the Aegean</a></li></ul></section>
